---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const lang = 'en';
---
<Layout
  title="Navigation: finding your way in the desert · CDM Motorsport"
  description="GPS tracks and reading the terrain: how to find your bearings on a motorcycle rally-raid in the Moroccan desert, at your own pace, without being glued to the screen."
  ogImage="/img/gallery/gallery-15.jpg"
  lang={lang}
>
  <section class="phero short" style="background-image:url('/img/gallery/gallery-15.jpg')">
    <div class="wrap c">
      <div class="eyebrow" style="color:#e8c9a6">Technique</div>
      <h1>The <em>navigation</em></h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pad">
    <div class="wrap article">
      <div class="meta reveal">Technique · 5 min read</div>
      <div class="big reveal" style="margin-bottom:24px">
        Navigation:<br />finding your way in the <em>desert</em>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <p>
          This is really what sets rally-raid apart from enduro: here, knowing how to find
          your way counts almost as much as knowing how to ride. And it's often what people
          end up enjoying the most.
        </p>
        <p>
          On my raids, I provide a GPS track that secures the whole route. It gives you the
          thread to follow, day after day, with no risk of getting lost in the middle of
          nowhere. But it doesn't ride for you: you stay in charge of your pace and your
          choices in the face of what the terrain throws at you.
        </p>
        <p>
          The real pleasure is learning to lift your eyes from the screen and read the
          landscape. Holding a bearing, spotting a pass in the distance, guessing where the
          piste runs from the color of the ground. It comes faster than you'd think, and it
          changes everything: you feel so much freer once you're not relying on the GPS
          alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          With me, that learning is part of the experience. I pass on the right instincts
          little by little, at your pace, and it's at the heart of my own journey as a
          rider. Nothing gives me more satisfaction than seeing someone gain real autonomy
          stage after stage. <a href="/en/edouard-de-moor">Who I am</a>.
        </p>

        <p style="margin-top:28px">
          Want to learn to find your bearings on the ground?
        </p>
        <a href="/en/reserver" class="btn dark" style="margin-top:14px">Book a raid</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
